<body><header class="hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Legacy Online School · Student project handbook</div><h1>Grade 6 Science<br>Earth in Action</h1><p>Investigating Our Changing Planet</p><div class="chips"><span class="chip">Module 2</span><span class="chip">Four groups</span><span class="chip">Two-week project</span><span class="chip">100 points</span><span class="chip">Student instructions</span></div></div></header>
<main><nav class="quicknav" aria-label="Page navigation"><a href="#start">Start here</a><a href="#projects">Find your group</a><a href="#workplan">How to work</a><a href="#submit">What to submit</a><a href="#rubric">Rubric</a><a href="#checklist">Final checklist</a></nav>
<section class="card" id="start"><div class="label">Read this first</div><h2>Your science team challenge</h2><p class="lead">Your team is a group of Earth scientists and designers. You will investigate a realistic problem, create an original scientific model or visual, and defend a solution using evidence from Module 2.</p><p><strong>Lessons you will use:</strong> Landforms · Weathering &amp; Erosion · Shaping the Surface · Spheres of Earth · Heat Transfer.</p><p>You have been assigned to <strong>one of four groups</strong>. Find your group below and complete its specific investigation. <strong>You do not need to complete the other groups’ projects.</strong></p><div class="note"><strong>Your overall goal:</strong> Explain a scientific problem, show evidence, make an original visual or model, and give a convincing conclusion. Your teacher will ask questions to check that every team member understands the science.</div><p class="small">Your teacher will confirm your team members, submission location and exact due date. Do not assume a due date from this guide.</p></section>
<section id="projects"><h2 class="section-title">Your group's assignment</h2><div class="quicknav" aria-label="Jump to assigned group"><a href="#group1">Group 1</a><a href="#group2">Group 2</a><a href="#group3">Group 3</a><a href="#group4">Group 4</a></div><div class="group-grid"><article class="card" id="group1"><div class="group-number">Group 1 · Coastal scientists</div><h2>The Disappearing Coastline</h2>
    <p><strong>Your situation:</strong> A coastal community is losing land because waves and moving water gradually remove soil and sand. Residents want to protect their homes and the nearby beach.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Your mission:</strong> Explain the changes and recommend a practical way to reduce erosion while considering possible effects farther down the coast.</p>
    <h3>Investigation tasks — complete all five</h3><ol class="task"><li>Explain weathering, erosion and deposition using examples connected to the coastline.</li><li>Identify at least three conditions that can increase coastal erosion.</li><li>Investigate at least two natural or nature-based methods of coastal protection.</li><li>Create a labeled model or diagram showing erosion, movement of sediment and deposition.</li><li>Recommend one strategy, explain why it could work, and discuss one possible limitation.</li></ol>
    <div class="bar"><strong>Your science model or visual:</strong> A before-and-after coastline diagram, physical model, or slide-based simulation with labeled arrows showing moving water and sediment.</div>
    <p><strong>Research evidence:</strong> Use at least one real coastline example or a reputable science source explaining wave action and sediment movement.</p>
    <p><strong>Challenge question:</strong> <em>Could protecting one section of coastline cause more erosion somewhere else? Explain your reasoning.</em> Prepare a reasoned answer for your presentation.</p>
  </article><article class="card" id="group2"><div class="group-number">Group 2 · Landform planners</div><h2>Landscape Designers</h2>
    <p><strong>Your situation:</strong> A community wants to build a settlement in an area with mountains, valleys, plains and plateaus. Some sites are steep; others flood or experience runoff.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Your mission:</strong> Compare landforms and propose the safest practical locations for housing, farming and roads.</p>
    <h3>Investigation tasks — complete all five</h3><ol class="task"><li>Compare mountains, valleys, plains and plateaus using elevation, slope and surface shape.</li><li>Build a labeled topographic sketch, relief model or digital landscape map.</li><li>Identify places where rainfall, erosion, runoff or slope movement could create problems.</li><li>Place homes, roads and farmland on your model and justify each location.</li><li>Explain how clearing vegetation or construction might change erosion risks.</li></ol>
    <div class="bar"><strong>Your science model or visual:</strong> An annotated settlement map with terrain features, a legend, at least two hazards, and clearly labeled building locations.</div>
    <p><strong>Research evidence:</strong> Include a source on topographic maps, runoff, slope stability or vegetation and erosion.</p>
    <p><strong>Challenge question:</strong> <em>Why might the flattest-looking land be a poor place to build?</em> Prepare a reasoned answer for your presentation.</p>
  </article><article class="card" id="group3"><div class="group-number">Group 3 · Earth-system analysts</div><h2>The Four Spheres Investigation</h2>
    <p><strong>Your situation:</strong> A factory near a river releases pollution into the surrounding environment. People notice changes in the water, plants and animals.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Your mission:</strong> Trace how pollution could move through connected Earth systems and recommend ways to reduce harm.</p>
    <h3>Investigation tasks — complete all five</h3><ol class="task"><li>Identify the atmosphere, hydrosphere, geosphere and biosphere with an example of each.</li><li>Explain at least six interactions or pathways between these spheres.</li><li>Build a connected diagram with arrows that show where substances or effects move.</li><li>Predict effects on river organisms, nearby plants, soil, air and people, distinguishing likely effects from uncertain ones.</li><li>Recommend three prevention or clean-up actions and explain the science behind each.</li></ol>
    <div class="bar"><strong>Your science model or visual:</strong> A systems-flow diagram with four clearly labeled spheres, six or more meaningful arrows, and a short caption for each arrow.</div>
    <p><strong>Research evidence:</strong> Support at least two pollution pathways with credible science information rather than guessing.</p>
    <p><strong>Challenge question:</strong> <em>If a pollutant enters only the river, how could organisms living on land still be affected?</em> Prepare a reasoned answer for your presentation.</p>
  </article><article class="card" id="group4"><div class="group-number">Group 4 · Climate-smart designers</div><h2>The Heat Transfer Challenge</h2>
    <p><strong>Your situation:</strong> Two houses stand in a hot environment. One stays relatively cool; the other becomes uncomfortably warm in the afternoon.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Your mission:</strong> Design a house that reduces unwanted heating and explain how energy moves into and through it.</p>
    <h3>Investigation tasks — complete all five</h3><ol class="task"><li>Explain conduction, convection and radiation using specific examples from a house.</li><li>Identify at least three ways heat enters or moves around a building.</li><li>Compare two choices of building material, roof treatment, shading or ventilation.</li><li>Draw or construct a house design and label examples of all three heat-transfer mechanisms.</li><li>Defend two design decisions and explain a possible trade-off or limitation.</li></ol>
    <div class="bar"><strong>Your science model or visual:</strong> A labeled house cross-section, physical model, or digital design showing sunlight, surfaces, airflow and thermal transfer.</div>
    <p><strong>Research evidence:</strong> Include a reputable explanation of one design feature (for example shading, a reflective roof or cross-ventilation).</p>
    <p><strong>Challenge question:</strong> <em>Why might a house with closed windows still become hotter when sunlight strikes the windows?</em> Prepare a reasoned answer for your presentation.</p>
  </article></div></section>
<section id="workplan"><h2 class="section-title">How to complete your project</h2><div class="card"><h3>Follow these steps</h3><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Meet your team.</strong> Write down each member’s name, agree on your group challenge and select roles.</li><li><strong>Plan the investigation.</strong> Decide what information you need, what you will create and who will do each task.</li><li><strong>Research reliable sources.</strong> Compare information, take notes in your own words and save source details.</li><li><strong>Create a scientific model or visual.</strong> Include labels, arrows, legends and units where needed; your model must explain the problem.</li><li><strong>Analyze and conclude.</strong> Connect the evidence to your findings; say what you predict and why.</li><li><strong>Prepare and rehearse.</strong> Create 5–7 slides, practise speaking and be ready for the challenge question.</li></ol>
<h3>Choose responsibilities</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Project leader</strong></td><td>Coordinates communication, deadlines, meetings and shared files.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Research scientist</strong></td><td>Finds and checks trustworthy science sources and explanations.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Model / data designer</strong></td><td>Builds the visual, model, diagram, graph or simulation and checks labels.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Science communicator</strong></td><td>Organizes slides and helps the group rehearse its explanation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="small"><strong>If your group has more than four students:</strong> Divide research, modeling and presenting responsibilities between members. Everyone must make a meaningful contribution and understand the whole project.</p>
<h3>Suggested two-week work plan</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Your task</th><th>What should be ready</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Week 1 · Day 1</td><td>Understand the task, divide roles and plan</td><td>Group plan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 1 · Days 2–3</td><td>Research and collect evidence</td><td>Notes and sources</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 1 · Days 4–5</td><td>Build diagrams, models, data or simulations</td><td>First draft visual</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 2 · Days 1–2</td><td>Explain findings and write your conclusion</td><td>Research summary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 2 · Days 3–4</td><td>Build slides, check accuracy and rehearse</td><td>Complete presentation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 2 · Day 5</td><td>Submit and present</td><td>Final work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="warning"><strong>Academic integrity:</strong> Use your own words and original work. Credit information, pictures, diagrams and data you did not create. Never present invented figures as real observations. If you use a simulation or estimated numbers, label them clearly. Ask your teacher before conducting any activity that could be unsafe.</div>
</div></section>
<section id="submit"><h2 class="section-title">Exactly what your group must submit</h2><div class="card"><ol class="task">
<li><strong>One presentation (5–7 slides).</strong> Include the project title and members, the problem, key science ideas, evidence, the model or visual, and your conclusion.</li>
<li><strong>One original scientific model or visual.</strong> This may be a diagram, map, graph, simulation or photo of a safe physical model. It can be embedded in the slides or attached separately.</li>
<li><strong>One research summary (200–300 words).</strong> Explain your question, method or research approach, strongest evidence, key findings, conclusion and any important limitation.</li>
<li><strong>One group contribution record.</strong> List every member’s name, role, tasks completed and how the team collaborated.</li>
<li><strong>At least two credible references.</strong> Give each source’s title, author or organization if available, and a usable link or publication details. Credit images and data too.</li></ol>
<div class="bar"><strong>Recommended slide order:</strong> 1. Title &amp; group · 2. Problem &amp; question · 3. Science explanation · 4. Evidence &amp; findings · 5. Model or data · 6. Solution or conclusion · 7. References / limitations (if needed).</div>
<p><strong>Suggested file names:</strong> <code>Grade6_Group[Number]_Presentation</code>, <code>Grade6_Group[Number]_Summary</code> and <code>Grade6_Group[Number]_Contributions</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Where to submit:</strong> Use the submission location your teacher provides (for example, the assigned Buzz activity). Submit one complete set of shared group files unless instructed otherwise.</p></div></section>
<section id="rubric"><h2 class="section-title">How your project will be graded</h2><div class="card"><div class="label">Assessment rubric · 100 points</div><p>Your team's science and presentation are assessed together, while your personal contribution and teamwork also matter. This prevents a group member from receiving full credit without participating.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Assessment criterion · What earns points</th><th class="points">Points</th><th>Scored as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Scientific accuracy and understanding</strong><div class="small">Your explanations use correct Module 2 concepts and show how processes work.</div></td><td class="points">25</td><td>Group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical thinking and problem-solving</strong><div class="small">Your team compares possibilities, makes justified predictions, explains limitations and defends conclusions.</div></td><td class="points">20</td><td>Group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Research quality and supporting evidence</strong><div class="small">Your claims are supported with relevant information from at least two credible sources, credited properly.</div></td><td class="points">15</td><td>Group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Creativity and contribution to model / visual</strong><div class="small">Your own contribution to an accurate, readable model, graph, map, experiment or simulation is clear.</div></td><td class="points">15</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collaboration and individual contribution</strong><div class="small">You complete your responsibilities, communicate with your team, and can explain the group’s work.</div></td><td class="points">15</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Presentation and communication</strong><div class="small">Your team communicates clearly, uses suitable visuals and responds accurately to audience questions.</div></td><td class="points">10</td><td>Group</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td>Total possible</td><td class="points">100</td><td>70 group + 30 individual</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p class="note"><strong>How scores work:</strong> <strong>70 points</strong> are shared group performance (accuracy 25 + thinking 20 + research 15 + presentation 10). <strong>30 points</strong> are based on each student's demonstrated visual/model contribution (15) and collaboration (15). Your teacher may ask you to explain any part of the group work.</p>
<h3>Presentation-day format</h3><div class="presentation"><div><strong>4 min</strong>Group explains the project</div><div><strong>2 min</strong>Teacher challenge question</div><div><strong>1 min</strong>Question from another team</div></div><p class="small">Each group gets approximately seven minutes. Practise so that all speakers are clear and the team stays within the time.</p></div></section>
<section id="checklist"><h2 class="section-title">Check before submitting</h2><div class="card"><p>Tick each box when your group has completed it. The checklist is saved only in this browser, on this device.</p><ul class="listchecks"><li><input type="checkbox" id="check0" aria-label="Completed: Our slides contain 5–7 slides, including a clear title and conclusion."><label for="check0">Our slides contain 5–7 slides, including a clear title and conclusion.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check1" aria-label="Completed: We have completed all five tasks for our assigned group."><label for="check1">We have completed all five tasks for our assigned group.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check2" aria-label="Completed: We created an original scientific model, diagram, graph, map or simulation."><label for="check2">We created an original scientific model, diagram, graph, map or simulation.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check3" aria-label="Completed: Our research summary is 200–300 words and explains the evidence and conclusion."><label for="check3">Our research summary is 200–300 words and explains the evidence and conclusion.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check4" aria-label="Completed: We cited at least two credible sources, including credits for images or data."><label for="check4">We cited at least two credible sources, including credits for images or data.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check5" aria-label="Completed: Our collaboration record lists every member, their role and what they contributed."><label for="check5">Our collaboration record lists every member, their role and what they contributed.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check6" aria-label="Completed: All members can explain the project and answer scientific questions."><label for="check6">All members can explain the project and answer scientific questions.</label></li><li><input type="checkbox" id="check7" aria-label="Completed: We have checked the work for accuracy, spelling, readable labels and originality."><label for="check7">We have checked the work for accuracy, spelling, readable labels and originality.</label></li></ul><div class="actions"><button type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save as PDF</button><button class="secondary" type="button" id="resetChecks">Clear checkboxes</button></div></div></section>
